      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "llegar",
      "answerEn": "to come to; eventually (in llegar a + action)",
      "noteEn": "Llega usually means “to arrive.” Before a and another action verb, it can instead present something as an eventual result.",
      "example": { "es": "Llegó a entender la idea.", "en": "She eventually came to understand the idea." },
      "tags": ["word", "verb pattern", "eventual result"]
    },
    {
      "id": "phrase-tal-y-como",
      "kind": "phrase",
      "promptEs": "tal y como + una afirmación",
      "questionEn": "How do you emphasize that something is exactly as someone said, expected, or described?",
      "answerEn": "Use tal y como before the description: it means “just as” or “exactly as.”",
      "noteEn": "Treat tal y como as one connector here; tal and y do not need separate literal translations. What follows states the expectation or description being matched.",
      "example": { "es": "Todo ocurrió tal y como dijiste.", "en": "Everything happened exactly as you said." },
      "tags": ["phrase", "comparison", "agreement", "B1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "phrase-por-parte-de",
      "kind": "phrase",
      "promptEs": "por parte de + persona o grupo",
      "questionEn": "How do you formally identify the person or group responsible for an action, judgment, or reaction?",
      "answerEn": "Use por parte de followed by the person or group.",
      "noteEn": "The expression often corresponds to English “on the part of” or, in some sentences, simply “by.” It highlights the source or responsible side rather than a physical part.",
      "example": { "es": "Hubo un error por parte del banco.", "en": "There was an error on the bank's part." },
      "tags": ["phrase", "responsibility", "formal", "B1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "phrase-al-igual-que",
      "kind": "phrase",
      "promptEs": "al igual que + persona o cosa",
      "questionEn": "How do you say that someone or something is like another person or thing in a relevant way?",
      "answerEn": "Use al igual que before the person or thing being used for comparison.",
      "noteEn": "The expression means “like” or “just as.” Al is historically a + el, but the full connector al igual que works as a unit in this comparison.",
      "example": { "es": "Al igual que Marta, Luis trabaja aquí.", "en": "Like Marta, Luis works here." },
      "tags": ["phrase", "comparison", "similarity", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "phrase-sin-duda",
      "kind": "phrase",
      "promptEs": "sin duda",
      "questionEn": "How do you say that you have no doubt about a claim?",
      "answerEn": "Use sin duda to mean “without a doubt,” “certainly,” or “undoubtedly.”",
      "noteEn": "The expression comments on the speaker's certainty. It can appear before or after the statement and does not change form.",
      "example": { "es": "Sin duda, ella conoce la respuesta.", "en": "Without a doubt, she knows the answer." },
      "tags": ["phrase", "certainty", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "grammar-forzar-a-alguien-a-infinitive",
      "kind": "grammar",
      "promptEs": "forzar a alguien a + verbo en forma de diccionario",
      "questionEn": "How do you say that someone forces another person to do an action?",
      "answerEn": "Use a form of forzar, a before the person, and another a before the unchanged action verb.",
      "noteEn": "The first a marks the person receiving the force. The second a links that person to the required action. The final verb stays in its dictionary form ending in -ar, -er, or -ir; only forzar changes for the person and time.",
      "example": { "es": "El jefe forzó a Ana a esperar.", "en": "The boss forced Ana to wait." },
      "tags": ["grammar", "verb pattern", "causation", "B1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "grammar-masculine-ma-nouns",
      "kind": "grammar",
      "promptEs": "el/un problema y otros sustantivos masculinos en -ma",
      "questionEn": "Why does Spanish say un problema even though problema ends in -a?",
      "answerEn": "Problema is masculine, so use el or un and masculine describing words with it.",
      "noteEn": "A noun's ending is only a clue to its gender, not an absolute rule. Several common words of Greek origin ending in -ma are masculine, including el problema, el sistema, el tema, and el programa. Learn each noun together with its article because not every word ending in -ma follows this pattern.",
      "example": { "es": "El problema es pequeño.", "en": "The problem is small." },
      "tags": ["grammar", "noun gender", "masculine", "A1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "concept-godwins-law",
      "kind": "concept",
      "promptEs": "la ley de Godwin",
      "questionEn": "What does “Godwin's Law” refer to?",
      "answerEn": "It is the observation that a comparison involving Hitler or the Nazis becomes increasingly likely as an online discussion grows longer.",
      "noteEn": "Mike Godwin coined the observation about internet discussions. It does not forbid every historically relevant mention of Hitler or require a conversation to end; treating it that way is a common comic exaggeration.",
      "tags": ["concept", "internet culture", "rhetoric", "humor"]
    },
    {
      "id": "phrase-maldita-sea",
      "kind": "phrase",
      "promptEs": "¡maldita sea!",
      "questionEn": "How do you exclaim “Damn it!” when something has gone wrong?",
      "answerEn": "Use ¡maldita sea! as a fixed expression of anger or frustration.",
      "noteEn": "The words literally resemble “damned be it,” but Spanish speakers use the pair as one exclamation. Sea is a special form of ser used here in a wish-like reaction. The expression can sound strong, so use it with care.",
      "example": { "es": "¡Maldita sea! Perdí el autobús.", "en": "Damn it! I missed the bus." },
      "tags": ["phrase", "exclamation", "frustration", "B1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "phrase-llegar-a-ser",
      "kind": "phrase",
      "promptEs": "llegar a ser + identidad o estado",
      "questionEn": "How do you describe someone or something eventually becoming a new identity or state?",
      "answerEn": "Use llegar a ser followed by the new identity or state.",
      "noteEn": "Only llegar changes for the person and time; a and ser stay unchanged. Ser alone names an identity or characteristic, while llegar a ser presents it as the eventual result of a change.",
      "example": { "es": "Con los años, llegó a ser médico.", "en": "Over the years, he became a doctor." },
      "tags": ["phrase", "change", "eventual result", "B1"]
    }
